WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.759
In 1903, the New York Times completely underestimated
how long it would take before anyone would

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:10.100
be able to fly, believing it would take 1
- 10 million years.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.759
Truth be told, even the Wright brothers didn’t
expect it to happen in their lifetime.

00:00:14.759 --> 00:00:18.810
The elder brother, Wilbur, lamented after
a failed flight: “Not within a thousand

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.830
years would man ever fly.”

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:25.029
Yet despite all this pessimism the dream became
a reality two years later.

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:28.609
In 1903, the Wright brothers made their first
powered flights above the outer banks of North

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:31.400
Carolina that lasted no more than a minute.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.940
Two years later, they improved their ‘flying
machine’ to such an extent that it was able

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.280
to fly 25 miles!

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.210
This is the story of how they achieved one
of the greatest innovations of all time.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.030
The Wright brothers had an interesting childhood
growing up in Dayton, Ohio.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.640
Their father, Milton, was a bishop.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.280
Their mother, Susan, devoted her life to looking
after her five children.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.290
It was she who fostered an interest in engineering
in her kids, having learned how to work with

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:59.180
tools and solve mechanical problems in her
own father’s workshop.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.610
Milton and Susan taught their children that
the world was an unfriendly place filled with

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:04.610
temptations.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.980
They believed the strength of family bonds
offered their only real support.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.510
It would help them face major crises, like
when twins born after Wilbur and before Orville

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:13.690
died in infancy.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.390
Milton’s preaching took him on the road
often, and he’d bring back small toys for

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.390
his kids.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.330
Once, he brought home a small model helicopter
based on a design by the French aeronautical

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.100
pioneer Alphonse Pénaud.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.370
This sketch was done by Orville.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:30.640
Wilbur and Orville were fascinated by the
toy and they developed a deep love of aeronautics

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.710
and flying.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.230
Wilbur was a bright student and excelled at
school.

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.690
He planned to attend Yale and become a teacher,
but while in high school, an accident changed

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:39.920
his life.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.050
When he was playing hockey, another player’s
stick hit him in the face, and he lost his

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:45.050
front teeth.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.540
Although his injuries weren’t severe, he
became withdrawn.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.870
And canceled his plans for college.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:54.310
He spent much of his time at home, caring
for his ill mother, who passed away from tuberculosis

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.910
a few years later.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:59.970
Despite the unraveling of his future plans
this would become the making of Wilbur.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:04.270
It freed him to pursue other interests which
would lead him to the airplane.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.750
The Wright brothers grew up during a period
of tremendous change in Dayton.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:12.170
It was emerging as the center of manufacturing
and industry – the perfect setting for two

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:14.200
entrepreneurial brothers.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.390
Wilbur and Orville started several local newspapers.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:19.830
And opened up bike shops at the peak of the
cycling craze.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:23.810
Their business renting, repairing, manufacturing,
and selling bikes would later financially

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:26.280
support their aeronautical experiments.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.060
Moving from bikes to planes was a natural
leap.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.920
Both require an understanding of balance and
control.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.980
The need for light yet strong structures.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.730
And the ability to deal with wind resistance.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:40.610
The earlier experimenters of flight thought
humans would never be able to handle air currents

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:44.400
– their reflexes just weren’t made for
it so they believed an aircraft had to be

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.650
stable in order for the pilot to maintain
control.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.819
However, the Wrights’ experience with their
bicycles taught them otherwise.

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:56.250
If a highly unstable object like a bike could
be controlled - why couldn’t a flying machine

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.320
be similarly tamed?

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.660
The brothers didn’t turn their sights to
the skies until German aviator Otto Lilienthal,

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:06.630
the first person to make repeated flights
with gliders, died when his glider stalled

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.810
and crashed in 1896.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.240
They were determined to develop a more successful
design.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.490
Despite humanity’s centuries-old interest
in flight, very little progress was made before

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:17.670
1800.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.750
That began to change in the 1800s, when a
community of technically trained people took

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.060
the first steps toward human flight.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:25.580
Wilbur was one of them.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.690
He wrote a letter to the Smithsonian Institute
in Washington asking for any publications

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:32.739
they had to help him gain knowledge of human
flight.

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:36.730
He said his purpose was to add his mite to
help on the future worker who will attain

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.090
final success.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:42.500
He didn’t think he’d achieve flight himself
but perhaps pave the way for someone else.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.050
The Wright brothers reduced the complications
they faced down to three essentials:

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:48.660
A set of lifting surfaces or wings

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.160
A method of balancing and controlling the
aircraft

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:55.380
A means of propulsion – the act of driving
or pushing forward.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.530
Most earlier experimenters focused only on
one of the three yet the Wrights realized

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:01.379
they all had to be addressed.

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:05.340
When they set out to design the wings, they
observed that birds angled their wings for

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:10.459
balance and control - they tried to emulate
this, a concept called “wing warping”

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:15.840
Their first experiments involved wing-warping
with kites, as the Smithsonian Museum explains.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:22.669
And this is how the Wright brothers, by maneuvering
their kite, discovered how to control an airplane

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:23.669
and roll.

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:25.689
It was one thing to build and fly a kite.

00:04:25.689 --> 00:04:29.289
Quite another to fly a large glider with a
person on board.

00:04:29.289 --> 00:04:32.860
They had to figure out how to climb and descend
which involves controlling the movement of

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.229
the center of pressure.

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:37.979
Lilienthal who died in the glider accident
mentioned earlier, controlled his flight by

00:04:37.979 --> 00:04:42.659
constantly shifting his body to adjust the
centre of gravity but this was impractical

00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:43.800
and dangerous.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.389
The Wright brothers thought they could stabilize
flight by mounting a horizontal wing-like

00:04:47.389 --> 00:04:50.629
structure called an Elevator on the front
of the wings.

00:04:50.629 --> 00:04:55.300
Pressure on the Elevator would counteract
the upward or downward movement of the aircraft.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.719
Designing the shape of the wing was also important.

00:04:57.719 --> 00:05:01.369
Although most wings had a perfect arc, the
Wrights chose a different design.

00:05:01.369 --> 00:05:05.369
They placed the highest point of the curve
closer to the leading edge and made the depth

00:05:05.369 --> 00:05:06.909
of the curve more shallow.

00:05:06.909 --> 00:05:10.789
They believed this would reduce the movement
of the center of pressure, making the aircraft

00:05:10.789 --> 00:05:13.050
more stable and easier to control.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:16.990
To test their glider, the Wrights headed to
Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, a small fishing

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:19.189
village known for its strong winds

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:21.199
and ferocious mosquitos!

00:05:21.199 --> 00:05:24.889
As Orville described: “They (mosquitoes)
chewed us clear through our underwear and

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:25.889
socks.

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:29.680
Lumps began swelling up all over my body like
hen’s eggs…Misery!

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:30.680
Misery!”

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:34.599
For all their hard work, their glider only
produced about half the lift that they hoped

00:05:34.599 --> 00:05:35.599
for.

00:05:35.599 --> 00:05:39.189
Lift is the upward force that overcomes weight
created by gravity in order to fly.

00:05:39.189 --> 00:05:43.449
They had only spent $15 to build the glider
- which is about $500 today.

00:05:43.449 --> 00:05:46.559
They vowed to try again with a bigger glider.

00:05:46.559 --> 00:05:50.710
The following year, in 1901, they returned
to Kitty Hawk with a glider that was twice

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:54.110
the size of their original only to come away
disappointed.

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:58.710
The larger glider performed worse than its
predecessor because it had problems with control.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:03.610
Wilbur felt so discouraged that he told Orville:
“Not within a thousand years would man ever

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.059
fly.”

00:06:05.059 --> 00:06:07.719
But history would prove otherwise.

00:06:07.719 --> 00:06:12.349
Shortly after, leading French-American engineer,
Octave Chanute, invited them to speak at the

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:17.370
prestigious organization Western Society of
Engineers to talk about their recent experiments.

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.930
Wilbur spoke about the problems he and his
brother encountered.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.880
The speech was well-received and lifted their
spirits.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:24.899
Back to the drawing board they went.

00:06:24.899 --> 00:06:29.270
The brothers suspected that their 1901 glider
failed because the data they used for calculating

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:30.610
lift was inaccurate.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:34.899
They questioned British engineer John Smeaton’s
coefficient of air pressure which had remained

00:06:34.899 --> 00:06:37.270
unchallenged for 150 years.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:42.589
The brothers determined Smeaton’s value
of 0.005 was incorrect and a value closer

00:06:42.589 --> 00:06:45.669
to .0033 would be more accurate.

00:06:45.669 --> 00:06:49.770
Lilienthal’s lift and drag coefficients
relied on Smeaton’s data.

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:53.379
But this still didn’t account for all of
the problems they had with their flights.

00:06:53.379 --> 00:06:57.289
The Wrights used their trusty bicycles to
test the accuracy of the data they had been

00:06:57.289 --> 00:06:58.289
using.

00:06:58.289 --> 00:07:01.589
They mounted a wheel to the handles of the
bike and rode through the streets of Dayton

00:07:01.589 --> 00:07:03.969
at various angles to obtain wind data.

00:07:03.969 --> 00:07:08.849
Their bike tests showed a much lower value
of lift than predicted by Lilienthal’s coefficients.

00:07:08.849 --> 00:07:13.029
Turns out, Lilienthal’s table only applied
to the specific wing shape he used in his

00:07:13.029 --> 00:07:14.029
study.

00:07:14.029 --> 00:07:16.369
The Wrights used wings with different aspect
ratios.

00:07:16.369 --> 00:07:19.689
To be sure their information was correct,
the brothers decided to better control the

00:07:19.689 --> 00:07:21.960
environment by building a wind tunnel.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.300
The wind tunnel was fairly simple in design,
consisting of a fan pushing a flow of air

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.300
through a box.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.740
It kind of looked like a coffin, at six feet
long.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.960
But the crude appearance of the tunnel belied
its sophistication.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:37.909
It was so good that it set the standard for
conducting aeronautical research to this day.

00:07:37.909 --> 00:07:40.960
They tested 200 different wing shapes in their
wind tunnel.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.029
At the end of their tests, they had the most
detailed data in the world for designing the

00:07:45.029 --> 00:07:47.509
perfect aircraft wings.

00:07:47.509 --> 00:07:52.679
In 1902, their new and improved glider performed
dramatically better than their two previous

00:07:52.679 --> 00:07:53.679
ones.

00:07:53.679 --> 00:07:59.169
In this photo from September 23, 1902, Wilbur
and Dan Tate, who helped with the glider experiments,

00:07:59.169 --> 00:08:00.819
are launching Orville in the glider.

00:08:00.819 --> 00:08:05.629
The 1902 glider allowed the Wright brothers
to gain extensive experience in the air.

00:08:05.629 --> 00:08:10.599
In September and October of 1902, they made
between 700 to 1,000 flights!

00:08:10.599 --> 00:08:14.469
Some even went as high as 600 feet or 183
meters.

00:08:14.469 --> 00:08:18.959
Their success gave them the confidence and
courage to try to invent a powered plane which

00:08:18.959 --> 00:08:22.929
they had earlier dismissed as impossible to
achieve in their lifetime.

00:08:22.929 --> 00:08:25.539
Wilbur was so excited about their progress,
he wrote:

00:08:25.539 --> 00:08:30.160
“Our new machine is a very great improvement
over anything…anyone has built.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.140
Everything is so much more satisfactory that
we now believe that the flying problem

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.180
is really nearing its solution.”

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.960
Their glider embodied the aerodynamic, control,
and structural requirements needed for human

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:41.960
flight.

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.990
The only thing it lacked was a propulsion
system to keep it flying.

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:50.250
In the summer of 1903, Orville and Wilbur
were consumed with overcoming this final,

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:51.780
momentous hurdle.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:55.770
The Wrights used a design inspired by their
kite to create their first plane which they

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:57.180
called the “Flyer”.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.240
To design their airplane, they used the data
they gathered from their wind tunnel experiments.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:05.210
They had to increase the wing area in order
to carry the weight of an engine, propellers,

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.500
and additional structural reinforcements.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.620
They built their own 12 horsepower engine
as nobody that manufactured gasoline engines

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:16.090
could meet their power and weight requirements,
or at least not for a reasonable price.

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:19.660
The engine’s job was to turn the two large
propellers at the rear of the plane.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:23.650
They drew on their knowledge of bicycles to
connect the engine to the propellers using

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:25.340
what amounted to “bike” chains.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:30.470
They returned to Kitty Hawk in September 1903,
determined to fly their airplane.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:33.820
But technical issues and poor weather made
them doubt themselves.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:38.140
The Flyer turned out to be 125 pounds heavier
than they anticipated.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:42.050
Fortunately, their propellers provided 50
percent more thrust than expected, which they

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.230
hoped would compensate for the extra weight.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:47.700
Still, were they crazy to try to fly an airplane
in 1903?

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.020
There was only one way to find out.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:55.550
On December 14, 1903, the Wright brothers
put their dream to the test.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:58.920
They tossed a coin to determine which brother
would make the first attempt.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:00.120
Wilbur won.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.760
The Flyer was only airborne for 3.5 seconds.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.340
But nonetheless, it did ‘fly’.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:10.040
Wilbur wrote confidently: “There is now
no question of final success.”

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:14.990
Three days later, on December 17, with the
Flyer repaired, they flew again.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:18.930
Moments before the flight with Orville at
the controls, a crewman observed an emotional

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.910
moment between the two brothers:

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:24.840
“After a while they shook hands, and we
couldn’t help notice how they held on to

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.220
each other’s hand, sort o’like they hated
to let go; like two folks parting who weren’t

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:31.270
sure they’d ever see each other again.

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:35.430
Wilbur came over to us and told us not to
look sad, but to laugh and hollo and clap

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.390
our hands and try to cheer Orville up when
he started.”

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:44.180
The Flyer lifted off - this time it stayed
in the air for 12 seconds and 120 feet (37

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:45.180
m).

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.360
Still, it was progress.

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:48.590
They would fly four times that day.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:53.380
The fourth and final attempt was the most
significant, as the plane soared to 852 feet

00:10:53.380 --> 00:11:00.000
(260 metres) lasting 59 seconds - proving
the Flyer was capable of a sustained, controlled

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.110
flight.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.780
After the flight, a gust of wind overturned
the plane, severely damaging it.

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.310
The Flyer wouldn’t soar again.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:09.850
But it didn’t matter - the Wright brothers
had succeeded in doing what they set out to

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:11.550
do.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.740
The brothers proclaimed: “…the age of
the flying machine had come at last.”

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:18.440
But they hadn’t yet created a practical
plane, one that could stay in the air for

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:20.110
a long period of time.

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:23.292
The 1903 flights were short and in a straight
line.

00:11:23.292 --> 00:11:27.900
They now had to prove they could fully control
it by turning and flying longer distances.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.670
Wilbur and Orville refined their design with
two more planes in 1904 and 1905.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:37.300
They got permission to fly by a local cow
pasture just a few miles outside Dayton, called

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:41.030
Huffman Prairie, so they didn’t have to
make the long trek to Kitty Hawk.

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:44.560
The owner didn’t charge them anything - he
only requested they make sure the livestock

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.030
were out of the way before taking off - so
as not to disturb or injure the poor cows!

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:53.400
On September 20, 1904, they flew their first
complete circle.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:59.210
The flight lasted 1 minute 36 seconds and
covered over 4,000 feet.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:04.040
By the fall of 1905, they routinely flew flights
lasting several minutes.

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:09.120
In one instance, Wilbur circled a field 30
times in 39 minutes for a total distance of

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:12.220
24.5 miles or 39 km.

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:16.500
The Wright brothers actually waited until
1908 to share their invention with the world

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:20.130
- they made sure to receive their patent for
the flying machine in America and several

00:12:20.130 --> 00:12:21.880
European countries.

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:24.780
Wilbur first flew their airplane in public
in France.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:29.260
The French aviator Léon Delagrange summed
up the superiority of the Wright machine,

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:31.790
saying simply: “Nous sommes battus.”

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:33.300
We are beaten.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:36.620
The Wright brothers became the archetype of
the “overnight success” story.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:40.490
When the Wright brothers returned to America,
they were greeted with much fanfare - including

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:42.090
a celebration in their hometown.

00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:47.020
Actually, they preferred quietly getting back
to work but just couldn’t escape the attention!

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:49.420
President William Howard Taft put it this
way:

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:54.370
“You made this discovery by a course that
we of America like to feel is distinctly American

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:56.840
- by keeping your nose right at the job until

00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:59.721
you had accomplished what you had determined
to do.”

00:12:59.721 --> 00:13:04.210
They made headlines in the fall of 1909 when
a million New Yorkers watched Wilbur pass

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:09.330
the NYC skyline during celebrations marking
the 300th anniversary of Henry Hudson’s

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.550
entry into New York Harbor.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:15.130
World War One was the great force behind manufacturing
aircraft.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:19.520
Planes were certainly a step up from the balloons
that the military used for observations during

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:21.170
the American Civil War.

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:25.230
In order to get a contract with the U.S. military,
the Wright brothers needed to prove their

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:26.770
plane worked.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:30.350
Unfortunately, one flight trial ended in tragedy.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:35.520
On September 17, 1908, a propeller flew off
the plane with Orville and passenger Lieutenant

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.350
Thomas Selfridge on board.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:42.160
The plane was 75 feet in the air when it took
a nosedive and crashed killing the Lieutenant

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:44.140
and seriously injuring Orville.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:47.570
Lieutenant Selfridge was the first person
to die in a powered plane.

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:51.060
The cause of the crash was a stress crack
in the propeller.

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:53.570
The Wrights redesigned the Flyer to eliminate
the flaw.

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:59.130
And later sold their Flyer to the Army for
$30,000 - the equivalent of around $927,000

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:00.220
today.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:04.750
The brothers formed the Wright Company in
1910 and began manufacturing a variety of

00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:05.750
designs.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:08.730
But Wilbur wouldn’t get much time to enjoy
his success.

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:11.850
Two years later, in 1912, he died of typhoid.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:15.370
He was only 45 years old.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:18.800
His family believes overwork contributed to
his death.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:22.630
Wilbur had been tired and stressed from the
lawsuits he and his brother filed for patent

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:23.630
infringement.

00:14:23.630 --> 00:14:26.900
They believed the invention of the plane was
their own and that they should be credited

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:29.690
and compensated for their contribution to
the world.

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:33.510
It was apparently not only a matter of money
but principle.

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:39.029
Tired and stressed from the burden of litigation,
Wilbur contracted typhoid fever in April 1912

00:14:39.029 --> 00:14:41.070
and died a month later.

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.900
His younger brother would live another 36
years.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:47.010
Orville spent the last three decades of his
life serving on boards and committees related

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:48.010
to aeronautics.

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:53.190
He died of a heart attack in 1948 at the age
of 76.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:54.680
The Wright brothers never married.

00:14:54.680 --> 00:14:56.340
They were wed to their work.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:01.221
To commemorate their unimaginable conquest,
statues sprung up nearly everywhere the Wright

00:15:01.221 --> 00:15:02.400
brothers had flown.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.370
The inscription on the memorial at Kitty Hawk
reads:

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:07.529
“Conceived by genius.

00:15:07.529 --> 00:15:11.370
Achieved by dauntless resolution and unconquerable
faith.”

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:16.190
As commercial aviation took off in the 1920s
and thirties, the airplane became a symbol

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:18.130
of a new, modern age.

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:20.590
The impact of their invention is beyond measure.

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:23.430
And also inspired us to soar to the moon.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:27.400
And now, the new dream to journey to Mars.

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:30.080
The Wright brothers were exceptional engineers.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:33.529
